CPSC 581 - Project 2: Expertise Sharing

An expert helps a novice with Blender. The expert sees a live picture of the
novice's Blender window and can:
  - draw over it (the drawing appears on the novice's screen)
  - place Blendy, a tour-guide avatar, on the novice's 3D model
  - plant numbered flags on the model; Blendy turns to point at each new one
  - chat with the novice
The novice hears a sound and sees a message whenever Blendy moves or a flag
is planted.

Requirements
  - Windows (the screen capture uses Windows-only libraries)
  - Python 3.12 or newer
  - Blender 5.2 or newer


Setting up
  In a terminal in this folder:
    python -m venv .venv
    .venv\Scripts\activate
    pip install -r requirements.txt


Running the demo
  1. Open "CPSC 581 - Project 2 - Test.blend" in Blender.
     Keep Blender open; it can be behind other windows but not minimized.
  2. Start the Blender listener:
     a. Go to the Scripting tab.
     b. In the Text Editor choose Text > Open... > blender_listener.py
        (the name box at the top of the Text Editor should now say
        blender_listener.py, not "Text". "Text" is Justice's original test
        script, which places a Blendy and a flag at a fixed spot.)
     c. Click Run Script (the play button), or press Alt+P with the mouse
        over the code.
     You can switch back to the Layout tab afterwards (it shows a bigger 3D
     view). The listener keeps running until Blender is closed. Running the
     script again just restarts it.
  3. In a terminal in this folder (with .venv active):
       python expert_gui.py
  4. In a second terminal:
       python novice_gui.py
  The line under the Avatar / Marker buttons says "Blender: connected" when
  everything is linked up.


Expert Window
  Brush / Eraser    Draw on, or erase from, the picture of Blender.
  Choose Color      Brush colour. Pen Size sets the brush and eraser size.
  Clear Canvas      Removes the whole drawing.
  Avatar            Click on the 3D view in the picture to move Blendy there.
  Marker            Click on the model to plant the next numbered flag;
                    Blendy turns to face it.
  Clear Avatar & Markers
                    Removes Blendy and all the flags from Blender.
  Chat              Type a message and press Enter or Send.


How it works
  Seeing Blender
    Both windows find Blender's main window and take a picture of it about
    30 times a second. If Blender isn't open yet, or is minimized, they show
    a message and carry on once it's back.

  Talking to each other
    The expert window, the novice window and Blender talk through small JSON
    files in %TEMP%\cpsc581_project2:
      stroke.json            the expert's drawing
      chat.json              chat messages
      blender_commands.json  expert -> Blender: "place Blendy / a flag here"
      blender_status.json    Blender -> both windows: what happened last,
                             plus a heartbeat showing Blender is listening
    The expert window clears these every time it starts.

  Drawings
    Lines are saved as positions on the Blender picture (0 to 1 across and
    down) instead of screen pixels, so they land on the same spot of the
    model in both windows, whatever size the windows are.

  The Blender listener (blender_listener.py)
    Runs inside Blender. Four times a second it checks blender_commands.json.
    For each new Avatar or Marker click it finds the 3D view under the click,
    shoots a line from the viewport camera through that point to find where
    it meets the model (or the ground), and places Blendy or the next
    numbered flag there, with a short animation. It writes the result to
    blender_status.json so both windows can show it, and the novice window
    plays a sound and shows a message.


FILES
  expert_gui.py        Expert window
  novice_gui.py        Novice window
  shared.py            Code both windows use: finding and capturing Blender, the picture canvas, chat, sounds, icons
  blender_listener.py  Runs inside Blender: places Blendy and the flags
  CPSC 581 - Project 2 - Test.blend
  Blender scene with Blendy, the flag and an example model
  icons/, sounds/      Button icons and sound effects


